  "name": "the indemnity",
  "description": "Chess in which losing a piece pays you for it. Every knight, bishop, rook or queen taken puts coins in the purse of the player who lost it, not the one who took it: one for a knight or a bishop, two for a rook, four for a queen; a pawn pays nothing. Instead of moving, a player may spend the whole turn raising a pawn for one coin, a knight for three or a rook for five onto any empty square of their own back rank, and each player starts with two coins. So the coins run against the lead: the side that wins material is the side that is not paid, and the side behind can buy part of it back, at the price of a tempo. The compensation is always less than the loss, a rook lost buying back less than half a rook, so trading down is never free, but an attack that strips the defender's pieces also fills the defender's purse, and has to land before the coins become a wall on the back rank. Bishops and queens are not for sale. A purse holds at most 99.",
